# 017 — Speech synthesis (TTS) on top of the audio gateway — done

- Type: feature · Priority: low · Status: done (2026-10-03)

## Problem
`audio_gw` accepted PCM and tones, but there was no speech synthesis; a client pushed a stream larger than 16 KiB by polling with sleeps.

## Done
- Kernel: `IPC_SAVE_REPLY` — a one-shot reply capability (not transferable), `IPC_REPLY` via the saved slot: a server can reply to a client later.
- `audio_gw`: `AUDIO_WAIT` — the client waits for free buffers, the reply comes from the AC97 interrupt; `mind::audio::Stream` and `wait_space`.
- The `tts` service (ring 3): a formant synthesizer (F1–F5 cascade, nasal pole/zero, parallel noise branch, KLGLOTT88 source, 16 kHz → 48 kHz stereo), Russian reading rules (palatalization, final devoicing, assimilation, «-тся», «-его», akanye/ikanye with heuristic stress) and Latin-script rules (rules + a lexicon of frequent words), phrase intonation.
- `mind::tts::say`, `say_with`; the `say` program reads `say.txt` from the disk.

## Verification
- `tests/qemu_smoke.py --suites tts`: duration and fundamental frequency of the speech recorded from QEMU; with `--asr-model` — word recognition by the offline Vosk model.
- `tests/tts_host.rs`: synthesis on the host to WAV and tests of the reading rules.
- Intelligibility with Vosk small: ~59 % of words on 30 Russian phrases, ~54 % on 8 English ones (it was 25 % before the formant transitions were reworked).

## Limitations
The voice is noticeably synthetic; stress is heuristic (no dictionary), so the reduction is sometimes wrong; English is read by simplified rules; pauses between words are lengthened for intelligibility.

## Audit (2026-10-04)

Done. Corrections: stress now comes from a dictionary (`data/stress_ru.txt`) and Latin script from a CMUdict-based lexicon (`data/lexicon_en.txt`); the current intelligibility figures are in the README.
